<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=crossbow">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crossbow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A crossbow is a bow mounted horizontally on a stock, shot by a trigger mechanism, allowing troops with limited training to shoot powerful bolts. Chinese armies used crossbows on a large scale from early times. They were standard weapons in the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and continued in use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crossbow.jpg" alt="Crossbow" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armborst,_1500-tal_-_Livrustkammaren_-_106793.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A crossbow is a ranged weapon in which a bow-like assembly called a prod is mounted horizontally on a frame called a tiller or stock. It shoots arrow-like projectiles called bolts or quarrels. Unlike an archer, who must draw and shoot in one quick motion, the crossbow user spans the weapon and cocks it, and a trigger-released mechanism then holds the string under tension. The weapon can be carried ready to shoot, the user has unlimited time to aim, and it can be used effectively with much less training than the English longbow or the bows of steppe nomads.</p><p>The drawbacks were weight and slowness. High draw weights required gears, windlasses, pulleys or cranequins to span the weapon, which were awkward on a battlefield, and medieval crossbows had short draw lengths, so they needed very high draw weights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known crossbows were invented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the first millennium BCE. Bronze crossbow locks dated to about 650 BCE have been found there, and bronze bolts from a Chu burial site date to the mid-5th century BCE. The Chinese trigger was made of three cast bronze pieces, which gave a mechanical advantage so that heavy draw weights could be released with a light pull. It shifted the role of projectiles in war, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification wars. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the gastraphetes, or belly-bow, appeared probably in the late 5th century BCE and was used at the Siege of Motya in 397 BCE. It was likely invented independently of the Chinese crossbow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>References to the crossbow in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> are essentially absent from the 5th to the 10th century. It reappears in 947 as a French weapon at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> and in 984 at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>. Crossbows were used at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066 and by the 12th century had become common battlefield weapons. European prods were wood until composite prods appeared in the 13th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> ones around the 14th, when winches and cranequins were also used. In the Islamic world the weapon was regarded as foreign, and Islamic texts do not mention it before the 14th century, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> cavalry used crossbows.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crossbow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Childeric’s grave at Tournai, found in 1653, contained a signet ring inscribed CHILDERICI REGIS and a Roman officer’s gold <b>crossbow</b> brooch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>crossbow</b>, fitted with standardised bronze trigger mechanisms, was the Han infantry's main technological advantage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At one battle against Northern Wei he is recorded as using spears launched by <b>crossbows</b>, which panicked the Wei cavalry and gave him a decisive victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Infantry used <b>crossbows</b>, pikes and gunpowder weapons including cannon and firearms such as hand cannons, while cavalry provided mobility and rapid response.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The main strength of the army was its heavily armoured knights, alongside hussars, light cavalry armed mainly with spears and <b>crossbows</b> for scouting, raiding and skirmishing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Qin armies of conscripted farmers, armed with <b>crossbows</b>, halberds and swords, then won repeatedly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 650 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Greece (the gastraphetes) (late 5th century BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some trace the crossbow and its name into China from Austroasiatic peoples in the 4th century BCE, but older locks from Zhou tombs contradict this.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Bow and arrow<span>c. 80,000 years ago</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">Catapult<span>5th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=crossbow">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
